staying at or something.

00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:12.300
I feel like they always have one.

00:05:12.300 --> 00:05:13.470
- [Craig] Yeah, that's happened for sure.

00:05:13.470 --> 00:05:14.823
I think that was in Austin.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.190
- Austin, Texas?
- Yes, yeah.

00:05:17.190 --> 00:05:18.900
- Oh man, they probably loved it.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.970
- Oh yeah, yeah. They came
around and was you know,

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:21.803
being silly.
- [Kelly] It's like

00:05:21.803 --> 00:05:22.636
Music City there.

00:05:22.636 --> 00:05:24.210
- We just did a whole
little show right there.

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:25.043
- What's the best,

00:05:25.043 --> 00:05:26.190
do you have like the
most memorable moment,

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:27.023
something that's happened like that?

00:05:27.023 --> 00:05:29.820
You just sat down and
something cool happened?

00:05:29.820 --> 00:05:31.680
- Oh.
(Craig laughing)

00:05:31.680 --> 00:05:36.420
I was at this party and
Mick Jagger was there.

00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:37.740
- [Kelly] What?
- And it wasn't like

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:38.910
Mick Jagger happened to be there.

00:05:38.910 --> 00:05:40.807
It was a party, we were doing this movie

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:41.820
"Get on Up".
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:45.780
- And they had a party at
this guy's house somewhere.

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:46.980
We were in Mississippi

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:48.810
and Mick Jagger's one of the producers.

00:05:48.810 --> 00:05:51.210
But then he got around the piano,

00:05:51.210 --> 00:05:52.770
was just singing tunes with us.

00:05:52.770 --> 00:05:54.810
- [Kelly] That's crazy.
- It was amazing.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:55.920
Wish I had that picture for you.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.680
- Oh my God, I swear, I
would've laughed while singing.

00:05:58.680 --> 00:05:59.513
Like, what is life?

00:05:59.513 --> 00:06:02.040
- [Craig] Oh, it was, yeah, it was just-

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:02.940
- [Kelly] I love moments like that.

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:03.773
It's like a movie.

00:06:03.773 --> 00:06:04.606
- Yeah.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:04.606 --> 00:06:06.030
- I was on a cloud.
- [Kelly] That's so cool.

00:06:06.030 --> 00:06:07.230
Have you always been into music,

00:06:07.230 --> 00:06:08.225
like since you were a
kid, is it something-

00:06:08.225 --> 00:06:09.120
you connect with.
- [Craig] Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:13.023
My mother was a musician.

00:06:13.860 --> 00:06:15.669
So she was first chair cello

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:18.048
in like, high school.
- Oh, I love the cello.

00:06:18.048 --> 00:06:20.370
- And she was a minister of music

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:22.050
and she sings like an angel.
- Yeah, yeah.

00:06:22.050 --> 00:06:23.618
- So music was in our blood.

00:06:23.618 --> 00:06:25.830
She was singing to us and playing

00:06:25.830 --> 00:06:26.970
for us when we were in the womb.

00:06:26.970 --> 00:06:28.830
- That's so cool, growing up
in that kind of household.

00:06:28.830 --> 00:06:29.820
Yeah.
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.365
- Is that, do you feel
like you connect with her.

00:06:31.365 --> 00:06:32.640
- And you'd have big Christmas parties

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.290
with everybody singing and-
- [Kelly] Yeah

00:06:34.290 --> 00:06:35.490
- Singing harmony and all that.

00:06:35.490 --> 00:06:37.320
- I love that. I know a lot
of people that did grow up

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:38.640
there in the music
industry and they grew up

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.590
with families that were
super musical like that.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:41.850
I wish I had grown up like that.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.020
That's so cool.

00:06:43.020 --> 00:06:44.670
And just, I loved harmonies.

00:06:44.670 --> 00:06:45.799
Harmonies are like my favorite thing.

00:06:45.799 --> 00:06:47.841
Like, 'cause I always
had to sing the lead.

00:06:47.841 --> 00:06:49.904
- Me too, me too.
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:06:49.904 --> 00:06:50.737
- [Kelly] It's fun to find it.

00:06:50.737 --> 00:06:51.653
- I don't always have to sing the lead,

00:06:51.653 --> 00:06:53.430
but harmony, it's nothing like it.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:54.471
- I know
- [Craig] It's nothing like it

00:06:54.471 --> 00:06:55.560
- It's so special to
connect with somebody.

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.100
Like, we don't think about it enough,

00:06:59.100 --> 00:07:01.140
but like when someone's making a sound

00:07:01.140 --> 00:07:03.330
coming out of their body and, like

00:07:03.330 --> 00:07:06.090
you do this note that
kind of vibes with them.

00:07:06.090 --> 00:07:08.490
It's just that feeling and
that energy is so special.

00:07:08.490 --> 00:07:10.578
I feel lucky that we get
to be a part of that.

00:07:10.578 --> 00:07:13.490
'Cause not everybody can find pitch.

00:07:13.490 --> 00:07:14.992
(Kelly laughing)
- And all the time

00:07:14.992 --> 00:07:17.889
we're like, you'll sing and
then they'll hop onto your note.

00:07:17.889 --> 00:07:18.722
- [Kelly] They'll help you out.

00:07:18.722 --> 00:07:19.555
'Cause they're-
- So you go to another note

00:07:19.555 --> 00:07:20.388
and they'll follow you to that note.

00:07:20.388 --> 00:07:21.221
- Oh my God, that
happened to me at a show.

00:07:21.221 --> 00:07:22.054
Somebody was like, let's do a duet.

00:07:22.054 --> 00:07:22.887
And I was like, awesome.

00:07:22.887 --> 00:07:24.411
And we got on stage and I was like, oh no.

00:07:24.411 --> 00:07:26.190
'Cause like, they couldn't-

00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:27.390
every time I was doing harmony

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:29.880
they'd just start singing
what I was singing

00:07:29.880 --> 00:07:32.220
and I was like you can't do that

00:07:32.220 --> 00:07:33.090
because, somebody's gotta

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:34.110
hold down the-
- That's called unison, yeah.

00:07:34.110 --> 00:07:35.730
- I know, but somebody's
gotta hold down the melody.

00:07:35.730 --> 00:07:36.990
I'm like singing alto over here.

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:38.670
Anyway, it was interesting.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:39.900
I think I realized at a younger age,

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:41.490
I was like, oh every singer can't do that.

00:07:41.490 --> 00:07:42.960
I didn't know that.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.744
It's interesting to find out
in front of 15,000 people.

00:07:45.744 --> 00:07:48.180
(all laughing)

00:07:48.180 --> 00:07:50.820
But we've got some of your friends

00:07:50.820 --> 00:07:52.200
up in Blake's box seats right now.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:54.000
The Harlem Globetrotters, what's up y'all?

00:07:54.000 --> 00:07:56.649
(audience cheering)

00:07:56.649 --> 00:07:58.982
- Zeus and Moose, what's up?

00:07:59.843 --> 00:08:01.920
So what is it that you're
doing with the Globetrotters?

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:03.180
What's happening?

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.950
- We're doing a show,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:07.440
it's called "Play It Forward".

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:10.950
